WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.300
What does a sensory nerve say about how hard you were touched?

2
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:15.920
In 1926, Adrian and Zotterman recorded one stretch receptor in a frog muscle.

3
00:00:16.820 --> 00:00:18.420
They loaded the muscle with weights.

4
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:21.400
The impulses never grew bigger.

5
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:23.600
They only came more often.

6
00:00:24.533 --> 00:00:27.353
Every sense starts with transduction.

7
00:00:27.933 --> 00:00:33.273
It turns a stimulus into a receptor potential, a change in membrane voltage.

8
00:00:34.073 --> 00:00:38.153
The potential is graded: a stronger stimulus makes a larger one.

9
00:00:39.133 --> 00:00:43.913
Where the ending belongs to the afferent neuron, that voltage drives the spike

10
00:00:43.913 --> 00:00:47.993
initiating zone. The larger it is, the faster the neuron fires.

11
00:00:48.883 --> 00:00:51.163
Endings also differ in time.

12
00:00:52.103 --> 00:00:57.583
A rapidly adapting ending fires at the start of a held stimulus, then falls quiet.

13
00:00:58.703 --> 00:01:00.583
Many fire again when it is removed.

14
00:01:01.963 --> 00:01:06.183
A slowly adapting ending keeps firing for as long as the stimulus lasts.

15
00:01:07.023 --> 00:01:11.343
So one reports change, and the other reports the stimulus itself.

16
00:01:12.317 --> 00:01:17.037
Plot firing against one stimulus, and you get a tuning curve.

17
00:01:17.777 --> 00:01:19.177
Here is a made-up neuron.

18
00:01:19.937 --> 00:01:24.777
It fires 50 spikes per second at 0 degrees, and 8 at 45.

19
00:01:25.617 --> 00:01:28.537
You might expect it to be most useful at its peak.

20
00:01:29.297 --> 00:01:32.897
For telling nearby stimuli apart, it is often the reverse.

21
00:01:33.737 --> 00:01:37.257
The steepest part lies between 15 and 30 degrees.

22
00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:42.557
With low, equal noise, a small change there moves the rate more.

23
00:01:43.497 --> 00:01:46.517
More noise can move the best place back toward the peak.

24
00:01:47.350 --> 00:01:50.430
Neighbours can also suppress a receptor.

25
00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:56.570
In the Limulus eye, lighting a unit's neighbours suppressed its steady discharge.

26
00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.510
Cutting the lateral connections abolished that inhibition.

27
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:03.270
Now try a simple sketch.

28
00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:09.550
Each unit reports its own light minus 0.2 times each neighbour's light.

29
00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:19.270
10, 10, 10, 20, 20, 20 becomes 6, 6, 4, 14, 12, 12.

30
00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:21.610
The edge is exaggerated.

31
00:02:22.550 --> 00:02:24.870
Rate tells you how strong.

32
00:02:25.750 --> 00:02:27.570
Receptive fields tell you where.

33
00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:30.310
Quality is the harder question.

34
00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:36.550
In a labelled line, a fibre's activity means one thing, such as sweet.

35
00:02:37.450 --> 00:02:42.370
In a population code, quality is read from the pattern across many fibres.

36
00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:48.910
In smell, one odorant is recognized by several receptors, so it is coded by a

37
00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:53.250
combination. Taste researchers still argue between the two.

38
00:02:54.250 --> 00:03:00.730
Keep this: spikes look alike, so meaning lies in their rate, their source and their pattern.
